good morning everyone on the 15th of  august we commemorate the 75th  anniversary  of bj day four months after ve day  and which marked the final end of the  second world war  a war which involved at least 25  countries  and resulted in between 55 and 59  million deaths  for the first time in a war military  casualties were outnumbered by civilian  casualties  being approximately two-thirds of the  total  in the pacific campaign the number of  civilian deaths  was in need in a higher proportion  although to many in great britain  it was far away and sometimes apparently  forgotten  the pacific campaign covers a large  proportion of  pacific motion south and southeast asia  from as far south as northern australia  to the aleutian islands in the north  the war ended following the decision by  the allies to use for the first time  atomic weapons at a dreadful human cost  and ushered the world into a terrifying  new age  nevertheless the use of those two atomic  bombs  ended the deadliest of all wars swiftly  with given the casualties already  incurred  in the pacific theater far fewer deaths  than any alternative so on the 15th we  commemorate  without as a result pandemic  any fanfare the end of a truly dreadful  conflict  and whilst the numbers of those who  remember the conflict are few  the sacrifice of those involved whether  military or civilian  in europe or asia must never be  forgotten  and must be an everlasting lesson of the  importance of dialogue and peace  rather than the alternative on behalf of  my fellow members and the staff of  stratfor district council  and the residents of this district let  us thank  all those from this area for their  sacrifices on this veggie day  and pray for peace christopher kettle  chairman of stratfor district council  thank you for listening  goodbye
 

